# Line endings and text classification for every tracked file.
#
# The rule: what is stored in git and what a fresh checkout writes must not
# depend on which platform the checkout happens on. M3 stores
# model data and compares it byte for byte, so a fixture rewritten in transit is
# a fixture that proves nothing, and the failure surfaces as a store test that
# passes on one machine and fails on another.
#
# text=auto lets git decide text from binary by content. eol=lf removes the
# platform from the answer for everything it decides is text: LF in the object
# database and LF in the working tree, on Windows as well as on Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The types tracked today, declared rather than detected. A declaration survives
# a file whose first bytes happen to look unusual to the detector.
*.md            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
DCO             text eol=lf
LICENSE         text eol=lf
NOTICE.md       text eol=lf

# Content that must never be line-ending converted, because a converted byte is
# a corrupted file rather than a reformatted one. None of these is tracked yet.
# The declarations are here so the first one added is safe on the way in rather
# than discovered afterwards, which is the case this whole file is about.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.7z      binary
*.gz      binary
*.AppImage binary
*.dmg     binary
*.exe     binary

# The CAD suite's own document format is a zip container. A line-ending
# conversion inside one produces an archive that no longer opens, and this
# project will carry them as fixtures from M3 onward.
*.FCStd   binary
*.FCStd1  binary
*.FCBak   binary
